---
title: Overview
summary: A tsdown plugin that ships a library's Svelte components the way svelte-package does, inside the same build as its TypeScript. Components ship uncompiled, with TypeScript stripped from the script and the template, a .svelte.d.ts beside each, and plain CSS copied.
related:
  - tsdown-plugin-svelte/options
  - tsdown-plugin-svelte/rolldown
  - reference/packaging
---

`@ogygia/tsdown-plugin-svelte` lets a Svelte component library keep one build. Your `.ts` goes through [tsdown](https://tsdown.dev) as usual, and your `.svelte` components ship next to it, the way `svelte-package` ships them, with no second build step:

| In `src/`       | In `dist/`                             | By          |
| --------------- | -------------------------------------- | ----------- |
| `index.ts`      | `index.mjs`, `index.d.mts`             | tsdown      |
| `Button.svelte` | `Button.svelte` (plain JS, uncompiled) | this plugin |
|                 | `Button.svelte.d.ts` (typed props)     | this plugin |
| `theme.css`     | `theme.css` (copied)                   | this plugin |

It works for any Svelte library, not only for packages used in ogygia apps: nothing ogygia-specific ends up in what you ship. ogygia's own packages are built with it.

- **Components ship uncompiled.** Each `.svelte` file lands in `dist` at the same path, as source. Your users' own Svelte compiles it, so your library never pins them to the Svelte version you built with.
- **TypeScript everywhere, plain JS shipped.** TypeScript is removed from the `<script>` and from the template: `{item as Row}`, `{user!.name}` and `{#snippet row(item: Row)}` are all fine. Your users need no TypeScript setup.
- **Typed props.** A `.svelte.d.ts` is generated beside every component with svelte2tsx, the engine `svelte-package` uses.
- **Safe stripping.** Types are found with Svelte's own parser, not with patterns, so a string or a comment that only looks like a type is never touched.
- **Styles come along.** Plain `.css` files under `src` are copied as they are.

## Install

```bash
pnpm add -D @ogygia/tsdown-plugin-svelte
```

It needs `tsdown` 0.15 or newer and `svelte` 5. For the defaults, also install:

```bash
pnpm add -D svelte2tsx typescript @sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte
```

`svelte2tsx` and `typescript` generate the `.svelte.d.ts` types; skip them with `dts: false`. `@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte` provides the default preprocessor; skip it by passing your own `preprocess` ([Options](/docs/tsdown-plugin-svelte/options)).

## A first config

```ts title=tsdown.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from 'tsdown';
import { svelte } from '@ogygia/tsdown-plugin-svelte';

export default defineConfig({
  entry: ['src/**/*.ts'],
  unbundle: true,
  dts: true,
  plugins: [svelte()],
  // the shipped components stay as files: never bundle them, or Svelte, into your JS
  deps: { neverBundle: ['svelte', /\.svelte$/] }
});
```

`unbundle: true` keeps one output file per source file, so `import Button from './Button.svelte'` in your `.ts` still points at the shipped component.

## package.json exports

Expose the components to Svelte-aware tools with the `svelte` export condition:

```jsonc title=package.json
{
  "type": "module",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "types": "./dist/index.d.mts",
      "svelte": "./dist/index.mjs",
      "default": "./dist/index.mjs"
    },
    "./Button.svelte": {
      "types": "./dist/Button.svelte.d.ts",
      "svelte": "./dist/Button.svelte"
    }
  },
  "files": ["dist"]
}
```

## With ogygia

A component library built this way is already in the shape an ogygia app needs. The app imports `my-lib/Button.svelte` with its own `wake` mark, and its build turns the component into a region; your package ships nothing for that. [Packaging](/docs/reference/packaging) covers the rest, including `ogygia.files` for marks that belong on your side.

